{{!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     navigation — the partial Ghost's {{navigation}} HELPER renders.
     ------------------------------------------------------------------------
     ⚠ READ THIS BEFORE WRITING {{#foreach navigation}} ANYWHERE ELSE. It does
     not work, and it fails silently — it renders nothing at all.

     `navigation` is not a variable in the page context. It is created by the
     {{navigation}} helper, which builds the array (adding `slug` and a
     resolved `current` to each item) and then renders THIS FILE with it:

         this.navigation = output;          // helpers/navigation.js
         _.merge(this, options.hash);       // …plus anything you passed in
         templates.execute('navigation', …)

     So the only place `navigation` exists is inside this partial and the
     partials it calls. Anywhere else, `{{#foreach navigation}}` iterates
     undefined and emits an empty string — no error, no warning, and gscan
     passes because it is valid Handlebars.

     `@site.navigation` IS available everywhere, but it is the raw setting:
     no `slug`, and no `current`. Both are needed here, so this is the path.

     ── ONE HELPER, THREE PLACES ─────────────────────────────────────────────
     The helper merges its own hash into the context, which is what makes a
     single partial serve three different renderings:

         {{navigation variant="bar"}}                    → the top bar
         {{navigation variant="sheet"}}                  → the mobile sheet
         {{navigation type="secondary" variant="footer"}} → the footer

     `type="secondary"` is the helper's own switch between the primary and
     secondary menus; `variant` is ours.
     ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ --}}
{{~#match variant "sheet"}}{{> "navbar/sheet-links"}}
{{~else match variant "footer"}}{{> "footer/links"}}
{{~else}}{{> "navbar/links"}}
{{~/match~}}
